  "notes": "The signature beat type the research names is \"a low ominous piano/bell ostinato over a static tonic with chromatic neighbour tones\", and the word doing the work is LOW. So the melody register here is 48–70 — the only entry in this batch whose lead sits below middle C, and the reason the \"huge empty mid-range\" the research describes actually appears: the lead is under the gap rather than over it. The chromatic neighbour tones are `halfStepDissonance` 0.22 and an `m2` passing tone on the 808, which only DaBaby otherwise uses and for the opposite reason. The rest is menace by weight: the batch's densest hats and highest roll frequency, aggressive glides on a heavily overdriven 808, and the flattest snare-roll descend (0.06) because the research describes big snare-roll transitions that climb into a guest verse rather than fall out of one. ⚠ `chords.impliedByRiff` is set — the research says a single dark motif with 3–5 pitches and no chordal pad. The i–VI family is INFERRED and carries the low weight; the research names only a static tonic with chromatic neighbours, so it is the first thing to delete if this ever sounds harmonic. `voicing.register` starts at 40 rather than the parent's 48 to match the low ostinato. ⚠ `arrangement.transitions.dropOutBeats` is 0.95 because the research says every guest verse is preceded by a full drum drop-out; the beats value is INFERRED. ⚠ `medium`: the research rates the flagship record medium-high but the catalog overall only medium. ⚠ `anticipationProb` raised to 0.15. With `mirror_kick` against a fully locked 808 both parts take every kick, so every bass onset lands exactly on an 808 onset — `coherence.rs` calls that one line played twice rather than two parts locking. The lock is the style and is untouched; the anticipation is what makes them two parts.",
